is.\n\n### Assumptions\nThe primary assumption of the Jarque–Bera test is that the sample data is independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.). This means that the observations should not be correlated and should come from the same probability distribution. The test also assumes that the sample size is sufficiently large, as the test's performance improves with larger samples due to the Central Limit Theorem.\n\n### Importance\nThe Jarque–Bera test is significant in econometrics because many inferential statistics, such as confidence intervals and hypothesis tests, depend on the normality of the residuals. If the residuals are not normally distributed, it can lead to unreliable estimates and incorrect conclusions. Therefore, the Jarque–Bera test serves as a diagnostic tool to validate the assumptions underlying regression models.\n\n### Interpretation\nUpon conducting the Jarque–Bera test, the resulting statistic is compared to a chi-squared distribution with two degrees of freedom. A high value of the JB statistic indicates a departure from normality, leading to the rejection of the null hypothesis. Conversely, a low JB statistic suggests that the residuals do not significantly deviate from normality, allowing researchers to proceed with their analysis under the assumption of normality.\n\n### Consequences of Violation\nIf the null hypothesis of the Jarque–Bera test is rejected, indicating that the residuals are not normally distributed, it can have several implications. The estimates of the regression coefficients may still be unbiased, but they may no longer be efficient, leading to larger standard errors. This can affect hypothesis tests and confidence intervals, potentially resulting in misleading inferences. In such cases, researchers may need to consider alternative methods, such as transforming the data, using robust standard errors, or employing non-parametric techniques that do not rely on the normality assumption.\n\nIn summary, the Jarque–Bera test is a vital tool in econometrics for assessing the normality of residuals in regression analysis. By evaluating skewness and kurtosis, it helps ensure that the assumptions underlying many statistical methods are met, thereby enhancing the reliability of econometric analyses.\",6\r\n637,OLS RESIDUALS,\"**OLS Residuals**\n\nOrdinary Least Squares (OLS) residuals are a fundamental concept in regression analysis, specifically within the framework of ordinary least squares regression. They are defined as the differences between the observed values of the dependent variable and the predicted values generated by the regression model. Mathematically, the residual for the i-th observation can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\ne_i = y_i - \\hat{y}_i\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(e_i\\) represents the residual for the i-th observation, \\(y_i\\) is the observed value, and \\(\\hat{y}_i\\) is the predicted value obtained from the OLS regression model.\n\nThe importance of OLS residuals lies in their role in assessing the fit of the regression model. They provide insight into how well the model captures the underlying relationship between the independent and dependent variables. A smaller residual indicates a better fit, while larger residuals suggest that the model may not adequately explain the variability in the data.\n\nIn the context of OLS regression, the residuals are used to evaluate several key assumptions of the model, including linearity, homoscedasticity, and normality. The analysis of residuals can reveal whether these assumptions hold true. For instance, if the residuals exhibit a pattern when plotted against the predicted values, it may indicate that the relationship is not linear, suggesting the need for a different modeling approach.\n\nFurthermore, the distribution of residuals is crucial for conducting hypothesis tests and constructing confidence intervals. If the residuals are normally distributed, it supports the validity of inference procedures based on the OLS estimates. Conversely, if the residuals are not normally distributed, it may lead to biased estimates and incorrect conclusions.\n\nIn summary, OLS residuals are the differences between observed and predicted values in ordinary least squares regression analysis. They serve as a diagnostic tool for evaluating the fit of the model and checking the underlying assumptions of linear regression. Understanding and analyzing OLS residuals is essential for ensuring the reliability and validity of regression results.\",3\r\n640,SKEWNESS COEFFICIENT,The skewness coefficient measures the asymmetry of the probability distribution of a real-valued random variable.,1\r\n641,KURTOSIS COEFFICIENT,The kurtosis coefficient measures the tailedness of the probability distribution of a real-valued random variable.,1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n610,JARQUE–BERA TEST,GUJARATI,Gujarati discusses the Jarque–Bera test in the context of econometrics in his textbook,270\r\n611,JARQUE–BERA TEST,OLS RESIDUALS,The Jarque–Bera test is based on the OLS residuals to assess their normality,9\r\n612,JARQUE–BERA TEST,SKEWNESS COEFFICIENT,The skewness coefficient is one of the measures used in the Jarque–Bera test,7\r\n613,JARQUE–BERA TEST,KURTOSIS COEFFICIENT,The kurtosis coefficient is another measure used in the Jarque–Bera test,7\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation